{"name":"Carved Pumpkin","hex":"#fb7d07","fetched":"2026-10-18","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"carved","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of carve"]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of an object, made by carving."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/carved"},{"term":"pumpkin","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A domesticated plant, in species Cucurbita pepo, similar in growth pattern, foliage, flower, and fruit to the squash or melon.","The round yellow or orange fruit of this plant."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pumpkin"}],"entries":[{"title":"Jack-o'-lantern","blocks":[{"text":"A jack-o'-lantern is a carved lantern, most commonly made from a pumpkin, or formerly a root vegetable such as a mangelwurzel, rutabaga or turnip. Jack-o'-lanterns are associated with the Halloween holiday. Its name comes from the phenomenon of strange lights flickering over peat bogs, called jack-o'-lanterns. It is suggested that the name also has ties to the Irish legend of Stingy Jack, a drunkard who, after deceiving people throughout his life and even Satan, is doomed to roam the Earth with only a hollowed turnip to light his way."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Balle-%C3%A0-leunettes_10.jpg/330px-Balle-%C3%A0-leunettes_10.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jack-o'-lantern","wikidata":"Q221009"},{"title":"Carving","blocks":[{"text":"Carving is the act of using tools to shape something from a material by scraping away portions of that material. The technique can be applied to any material that is solid enough to hold a form even when pieces have been removed from it, and yet soft enough for portions to be scraped away with available tools. Carving, as a means for making stone or wooden sculpture, is distinct from methods using soft and malleable materials like clay, fruit, and melted glass, which may be shaped into the desired forms while soft and then harden into that form. Carving tends to require much more work than methods using malleable materials."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/LDDilwaraCeiling.jpg/330px-LDDilwaraCeiling.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carving","wikidata":"Q13039303"},{"title":"Pumpkin","blocks":[{"text":"A pumpkin is a cultivated winter squash in the genus Cucurbita. The term is most commonly applied to round, orange-colored squash varieties, but does not possess a scientific definition. It may be used in reference to many different squashes of varied appearance and belonging to multiple species in the Cucurbita genus."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/FrenchMarketPumpkinsB.jpg/330px-FrenchMarketPumpkinsB.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pumpkin","wikidata":"Q165308"},{"title":"Shades of orange","blocks":[{"text":"In optics, orange has a wavelength between approximately 585 and 620 nm and a hue of 30° in HSV color space. In the RGB color space it is a secondary color numerically halfway between gamma-compressed red and yellow, as can be seen in the RGB color wheel. The complementary color of orange is azure. Orange pigments are largely in the ochre or cadmium families, and absorb mostly blue light."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Shades_of_Orange.svg/330px-Shades_of_Orange.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_orange","wikidata":"Q19847194"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Balle-%C3%A0-leunettes_10.jpg/500px-Balle-%C3%A0-leunettes_10.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Balle-%C3%A0-leunettes_10.jpg/500px-Balle-%C3%A0-leunettes_10.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A traditional American jack-o'-lantern, made from a pumpkin, lit from within by a candle","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABalle-%C3%A0-leunettes_10.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/RIP_Pumpkin.JPG/500px-RIP_Pumpkin.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/RIP_Pumpkin.JPG/1280px-RIP_Pumpkin.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/RIP_Pumpkin.JPG/500px-RIP_Pumpkin.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A picture carved onto a jack-o'-lantern for Halloween","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARIP_Pumpkin.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Traditional_Irish_halloween_Jack-o%27-lantern.jpg/500px-Traditional_Irish_halloween_Jack-o%27-lantern.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Traditional_Irish_halloween_Jack-o%27-lantern.jpg/500px-Traditional_Irish_halloween_Jack-o%27-lantern.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A plaster cast of a traditional Irish jack-o'-lantern in the Museum of Country Life, Ireland.  Rutabaga or turnip were often used.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATraditional_Irish_halloween_Jack-o'-lantern.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Thanksgiving_Greetings%2C_jack-o-lantern_and_a_turkey_%28NBY_18516%29.jpg/500px-Thanksgiving_Greetings%2C_jack-o-lantern_and_a_turkey_%28NBY_18516%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_